@startuml
title Vapor Publication Model

start

partition "Local Source / Git" {
    :Author Vapor Content / Pack source;
    :Validate local authored source;
    :Commit intended publication state;
}

partition "Remote Git / Source Publication" {
    :Push / publish source;
    :Establish retrievable source version;
}

partition "Vapor Content Registry" {
    :Resolve / register Vapor identity;
    :Link semantic identity to source location/version;
}

partition "Composition Build" {
    :Resolve Packagepack;
    :Resolve exact required source;
    :Validate complete composition;
    :Build target-specific Vapor App artifact(s);
    :Validate distribution artifact(s);
}

partition "Steam Workshop" {
    :Publish built complete composition;
}

partition "Vapor Content Registry" {
    :Link Vapor publication identity
    to built Workshop distribution;
    :Expose publication for discovery;
}

partition "Player" {
    :Discover Vapor App;
    :Acquire compatible built artifact;
    :Install / register locally;
    :Select / launch;
}

stop

note right
This diagram shows the broad
successful publication direction.

It is not an atomicity or failure
state machine.

Source publication, Registry linkage,
build, Workshop publication, and
final Registry publication state can
succeed or fail independently and
must eventually support recovery.
end note

@enduml